WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.990
Film Courage: Potential pitfalls or traps a
screenwriter should be aware of when

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:13.380
trying to sell their screenplay pitfalls?
Kathie Fong Yoneda: Hhmm...I think the biggest one is don't try

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:19.080
and make your project be one of those
where you're forcing trying to force it

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.080
into being what they think the studio
wants or what that production company

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.380
wants that's a big pitfall for me
because then somewhere along the line I

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:38.700
think you sometimes will lose the heart
of what your project is I know I know a

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.350
lot of times they they will ask for a
request to do something to it and you

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:48.600
you do need to if they bought it it is
their project and so I think a lot of

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.550
writers sometimes immediately are very
protective of their project and they

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.510
don't and they say well yeah I'll sell
it to you but you can't do this or you

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:02.160
know I mean that's sort of a first
reaction what they have to understand is

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.030
that they've got stockholders usually
that they have to answer to and and

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:11.490
there's when money is involved there are
some changes they have to make I think

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.909
when they when you are just in the
talking stages sometimes they make

00:01:16.909 --> 00:01:22.259
writers make the biggest mistake is when
they start being too too concerned about

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:28.020
all of that and I want them to
understand it's good to listen first to

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.750
what they have to say before reacting
immediately and jumping all over it I

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:38.750
think you should need to listen to what
people have to say and then talk it over

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.829
with your agent or talking over with the
creative exec who's there which is the

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:48.000
kind of work I used to do and a lot of
times I had to explain to him the reason

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.880
why they asked for a certain change
maybe it was because a budget a lot of

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:00.600
times it's because maybe they got a
director and the director maybe didn't

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:06.750
like a certain aspect of it or maybe it
was an actress or an actor and maybe

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:12.750
their acting range doesn't cover
sufficiently the emotional impact of a

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.660
certain scene
I mean there's a lot of reasons that

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:20.280
that people don't understand about it so
if you are fortunate enough that

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.840
somebody is optioning or buying your
project and they are asking for things

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.980
it's not because they don't like it they
want to see it get made and sometimes

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.580
it's because of certain reasons and you
need to sit down and talk to somebody

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:40.080
about it don't just immediately get you
know all up in arms and just I know I'm

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.580
not gonna do that and I hate this I did
you know listen to what people have to

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:49.860
say sometimes it's something that says a
small enough thing and there's sometimes

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:54.600
there's this way she can compromise half
of my job when I was a creative because

00:02:54.600 --> 00:03:01.530
that was trying to find the best way to
still keep the project there as the

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:07.560
writer's vision as well but sometimes if
you have a director who's a big name you

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.880
have to waive the consequences do we do
you want to keep the big are they going

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.590
to keep the big name director or the
first-time writer and it's a real easy

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.970
thing for the studio it's if they figure
we've already bought it we don't need

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.840
the writer to come back and do the
rewrite and you want to stay in business

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:31.200
with people so you have to learn when to
to fight and when to listen to what's

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.870
being said and when to give in you know
and it doesn't mean you completely given

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:40.710
talk to the you talk to the creative
because usually there's at least two

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:44.940
execs there's the the usually there's a
VP and usually there's a creative exec

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:49.350
talk to the creative Kazaam find out
their reasons why and see if there's

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:54.690
some way that there can be some kind of
a compromise to it and a lot of times

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.320
that was that like I said that's that
was a great deal of my of the work that

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.690
I did was working with the writers and I
think that's why I was a successful as I

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:07.590
was is that the writers liked the fact
that I would talk with them and I wasn't

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.930
afraid to say well okay here are some
that here are some options we can do

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:18.090
which of these options do you think fits
your story best if I would give them

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:23.760
options then they felt they were more in
control I wanted to give that to them so

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.980
they wouldn't feel that everything was
lost to them

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:33.280
I think sometimes executives have a way
of being very to-the-point and very

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:37.840
Bruce and okay we need to change this
scene doesn't work and sometimes they

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:45.190
don't explain why that's part of what a
creative exec does and so I think that

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:48.970
that's something that you know if you're
not sure if writers are not sure they

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.810
should talk to somebody and just say can
you explain that to me in a little more

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.950
detail because I wasn't quite sure what
it means that it doesn't work exactly

00:04:56.950 --> 00:05:03.100
what is it about it that it didn't work
so I don't know if that I hope that that

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.150
helps because I do I do feel that there
have been a lot of writers who have lost

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:11.590
an opportunity to have their work
produced because they immediately jumped

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.010
up and they just said no I won't do it
because they were too precious with it

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:19.660
mm-hmm and they didn't even want to
listen to what you know the explanations

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.050
or anything

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.150
you

